Important information included here is the time, filename and the outcome of a scan (under **Scan result**).

Scan results are either **Clean** or **Infected**.

If a file is **Clean** it moves to the `processed` bucket prior to the `transfer` lambda transferring it to the destination bucket.

> **Note**
>
> If **Known viruses** is **0** check the `definition-upload` function logs.

## Checking Transfer Family server logs

In the unlikely event that you need to look at transfer server logs these are `/aws/transfer-structured-logs` in CloudWatch.
These can be used to verify connection times and user activity on the server.

## Reproducing Transfer Family user issues

**Purpose:** reproduce a user’s issue by temporarily logging in as that user using your own SSH key and your IP, while inheriting the same IAM role and permissions.

You may achieve similar results by [using our team user `analytical-platform`](#using-the-analytical-platform-user).

1. Add your SSH public key to the user in question.

   - In the user resource in the AWS console, **Transfer Family** > **Servers** > `{server-name}` > **User** – add your SSH public key so the same username accepts your key.

2. Allow your IP address on the Transfer Family server’s security group.

   - Find the security group associated with the server:

     **EC2** > **Security Groups**

     - **Name:** `analytical-platform-ingestion-<environment>-transfer-server`
     - **Description:** `Security Group for Transfer Server`

   - Edit inbound rules for the Security Group and add an inbound rule for your current public IP address on the appropriate security group, adding it in IPv4 CIDR block `/32` notation to indicate a single address.

3. Connect exactly as the user.

   - Use the same endpoint and username the user does.
   - Connect with SFTP using your private key that matches the public key added in step 1.

   Production:

   ```shell
   sftp -P 2222 \
     ${USERNAME}@sftp.ingestion.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
   ```

   Development:

   ```shell
   sftp -P 2222 \
     ${USERNAME}@sftp.development.ingestion.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
   ```

   - Verify login.
   - Run basic commands (for example `pwd`, `ls`, `put`). Note that once the Lambda kicks in, any transferred files are deleted.

   ```shell
   sftp> pwd
   Remote working directory: /mojap-ingestion-development-landing/maat-xhibit-dev

   sftp> ls

   sftp> put /analytical/work/test_sftp.txt
   Uploading /analytical/work/test_sftp.txt to \
   /mojap-ingestion-development-landing/maat-xhibit-dev/test_sftp.txt

   test_sftp.txt
   100% 0 0.0KB/s 00:00

   sftp> ls
   test_sftp.txt

   sftp> ls
   ```

   - Run `put` to upload a file and confirm that it arrives in the destination.

   This can be done in the console:

   - Navigate to the Lambda called `transfer`.
   - Select the **Monitor** tab, then **View CloudWatch Logs**, and find the latest log stream.
   - In the log stream, look for an entry similar to:

     ```text
     Successfully copied maat-xhibit-dev/test_sftp.txt \
     to mojap-land-dev/laa/dev/maatxhibit/test_sftp.txt
     ```

     or check the destination bucket in `analytical-platform-data-platform`, in this case:

     ```text
     mojap-land-dev/laa/dev/maatxhibit/
     ```

4. Reproduce the reported behaviour.

   - Perform the same operations (upload, list, download) using the same filenames and paths the user reported.
   - Capture outputs and errors to validate hypotheses (for example filename handling or permissions).

5. (If required) Iterate fixes while impersonating.

   - Adjust only what’s necessary (for example S3/IAM permissions or filename conventions) and re-test under the impersonated session until behaviour matches expectations.

6. Cleanup (**mandatory**).

   - Remove your SSH key from the user configuration.
   - Remove your IP from the security group.
   - Reconcile any temporary manual edits back into code (or revert them) so the environment returns to its prior state.

### Notes

- This method deliberately does **not** create a new Transfer Family user.
- All access is temporary and auditable via code changes and security group updates.
- Because you connect with the same username and IAM role, you reproduce the user’s effective permissions and pathing precisely.

## Using the `analytical-platform` user

Use the `analytical-platform` user to connect to the development Transfer Family server.

1. Allow your IP address to connect to the server.

   Update the security group to allow connections from your IP address. See step 2 in [Reproducing Transfer Family user issues](#reproducing-transfer-family-user-issues).

2. Download the private key.

   You can find the [private key for the `analytical-platform` user in 1Password](https://start.1password.com/open/i?a=WEXD5VMFTVBH7LG7FFDWUV7MC4&v=skgdudwgk3ojqiwigoxrmpngle&i=l42to46jqwj5d2lri6e5ipuuya&h=ministryofjustice.1password.eu).

   Download the key and save it to a file called `id_rsa`.

3. Connect to the development server.

   From the directory containing `id_rsa`, run:

   ```shell
   sftp -P 2222 -i id_rsa analytical-platform@sftp.development.ingestion.analytical-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
   ```

   The `-i` option specifies the path to the private key. In the example above, the key is in the current working directory. If you saved the key somewhere else, specify the relative path to the key instead.

   > **Note**
   >
   > If you already have a private key in `~/.ssh/`, the command will attempt to use this unless you specify the `analytical-platform` key using the `-i` option.

